WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.438
Public Speaking.

2
00:00:01.718 --> 00:00:05.814
Day 20 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Communication phase.

3
00:00:07.564 --> 00:00:08.588
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:18.873
Today's goal: plan and deliver a short talk (a demo, a team update, a meetup talk or a speech at an event) with a clear structure, a steady voice and less fear.

5
00:00:20.223 --> 00:00:21.077
Part 1.

6
00:00:21.357 --> 00:00:23.149
Fear is normal: use it.

7
00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:27.577
Almost everyone feels nervous before speaking to a group.

8
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.033
Your heart beats faster to give you energy.

9
00:00:30.313 --> 00:00:35.071
The goal isn't to remove the nerves; it's to prepare so well that the nerves don't matter.

10
00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:37.377
Nobody can see how nervous you feel.

11
00:00:37.657 --> 00:00:39.300
You look much calmer than you think.

12
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.351
The audience wants you to succeed.

13
00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:43.700
The first 30 seconds are the hardest.

14
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.433
Memorise your opening and you'll get through them.

15
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:48.658
Part 2.

16
00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:50.474
The simple talk structure.

17
00:00:51.724 --> 00:00:52.641
Step 1:

18
00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:57.081
Hook: a question, a surprising number or a short story (30 s).

19
00:00:57.361 --> 00:00:58.279
Step 2:

20
00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:01.119
Point: what this talk is about, in one sentence.

21
00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:02.316
Step 3:

22
00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:06.649
Three parts: no more than three main ideas, each with one example.

23
00:01:06.929 --> 00:01:07.889
Step 4:

24
00:01:08.169 --> 00:01:10.793
Recap: repeat the three ideas in one line.

25
00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:12.033
Step 5:

26
00:01:12.313 --> 00:01:15.684
Close: a call to action or a memorable final sentence.

27
00:01:17.034 --> 00:01:17.909
Example:

28
00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:19.639
A 5-minute team talk.

29
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:20.559
Hook:

30
00:01:20.839 --> 00:01:24.146
Last month, we spent 31 hours waiting for code reviews.

31
00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:25.130
Point:

32
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.333
Today I'll share three changes that cut that in half.

33
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:29.402
Parts:

34
00:01:29.682 --> 00:01:30.322
1.

35
00:01:30.602 --> 00:01:32.010
Smaller PRs, 2.

36
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.719
A review rota, 3.

37
00:01:33.999 --> 00:01:35.898
Review labels (nit, blocker).

38
00:01:36.178 --> 00:01:36.925
Recap:

39
00:01:37.205 --> 00:01:39.423
Small PRs, a rota, and labels.

40
00:01:39.703 --> 00:01:40.514
Close:

41
00:01:40.794 --> 00:01:42.437
Let's try it for two sprints.

42
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:44.765
I'll share the numbers at the next retro.

43
00:01:46.115 --> 00:01:47.011
Part 3.

44
00:01:47.291 --> 00:01:49.104
Phrases that guide your audience.

45
00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:51.719
Here is a quick table:

46
00:01:51.999 --> 00:01:53.343
Purpose, and Phrases.

47
00:01:53.623 --> 00:01:54.434
Opening:

48
00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.229
Good morning, everyone.

49
00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:59.751
Thank you for having me. / Let me start with a question.

50
00:02:00.031 --> 00:02:00.927
Agenda:

51
00:02:01.207 --> 00:02:04.834
Today I'll cover three things: first... second... and finally...

52
00:02:05.114 --> 00:02:06.031
Moving on:

53
00:02:06.311 --> 00:02:09.618
That brings me to my second point. / Now let's look at...

54
00:02:09.898 --> 00:02:10.773
Example:

55
00:02:11.053 --> 00:02:13.613
For example... / Let me show you what I mean.

56
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:14.789
Emphasis:

57
00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:19.399
The most important thing is... / If you remember one thing today, it's this.

58
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:20.426
Recap:

59
00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:23.479
So, to sum up... / Let's quickly recap.

60
00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:24.570
Closing:

61
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.109
Thank you for listening.

62
00:02:26.389 --> 00:02:28.053
I'm happy to take questions.

63
00:02:29.403 --> 00:02:30.320
Part 4.

64
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.731
Voice and body.

65
00:02:32.981 --> 00:02:34.346
Here is a quick table:

66
00:02:34.626 --> 00:02:35.650
Do, and Avoid.

67
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.999
Speak 20% slower than normal:

68
00:02:38.279 --> 00:02:39.773
Rushing to get it over with.

69
00:02:40.053 --> 00:02:41.695
Pause after important points:

70
00:02:41.975 --> 00:02:44.471
Fillers: um, basically, actually, like.

71
00:02:44.751 --> 00:02:47.098
Look at different people, one sentence each:

72
00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:49.255
Reading from slides or your phone.

73
00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:52.565
Stand with feet apart, hands relaxed at waist level:

74
00:02:52.845 --> 00:02:55.191
Hands in pockets, crossed arms, swaying.

75
00:02:55.471 --> 00:02:57.861
Vary your voice: louder for key points:

76
00:02:58.141 --> 00:02:59.933
One flat tone for the whole talk.

77
00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:01.941
Smile at the start and the end:

78
00:03:02.221 --> 00:03:03.351
Apologising:

79
00:03:03.631 --> 00:03:05.445
Sorry, I'm not a good speaker...

80
00:03:06.795 --> 00:03:07.755
Part 5.

81
00:03:08.035 --> 00:03:09.613
Preparing in 5 steps.

82
00:03:10.863 --> 00:03:13.850
Write your three main points and one example for each.

83
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.687
Say it out loud three times.

84
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:18.421
Don't write a script to memorise word for word.

85
00:03:18.701 --> 00:03:22.263
Record yourself once on your phone and watch it at normal speed.

86
00:03:22.543 --> 00:03:23.311
Time it.

87
00:03:23.591 --> 00:03:25.170
Cut anything that goes over.

88
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.162
Practise the first and last 30 seconds until they're automatic.

89
00:03:30.512 --> 00:03:31.451
Part 6.

90
00:03:31.731 --> 00:03:33.011
Handling questions.

91
00:03:34.261 --> 00:03:35.626
Here is a quick table:

92
00:03:35.906 --> 00:03:37.293
Situation, and Say.

93
00:03:37.573 --> 00:03:38.575
Good question:

94
00:03:38.855 --> 00:03:39.943
Great question.

95
00:03:40.223 --> 00:03:42.378
Then answer in 2 to 3 sentences.

96
00:03:42.658 --> 00:03:43.554
You don't know:

97
00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:47.375
I don't know, but I'll find out and share it with everyone by tomorrow.

98
00:03:47.655 --> 00:03:49.213
Long, unclear question:

99
00:03:49.493 --> 00:03:52.394
Just to make sure I understand: you're asking about...?

100
00:03:52.674 --> 00:03:53.634
Off-topic:

101
00:03:53.914 --> 00:03:55.045
Interesting point.

102
00:03:55.325 --> 00:03:57.117
Can we discuss it after the session?

103
00:03:57.397 --> 00:03:58.293
Silence:

104
00:03:58.573 --> 00:04:00.343
A question I often get is...

105
00:04:00.623 --> 00:04:01.903
(answer it yourself).

106
00:04:03.253 --> 00:04:04.192
Part 7.

107
00:04:04.472 --> 00:04:06.797
Conversation: preparing with a colleague.

108
00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.074
Here is how the conversation goes.

109
00:04:10.354 --> 00:04:11.997
Do you have five minutes?

110
00:04:12.277 --> 00:04:16.245
I'm presenting at the all-hands tomorrow, and I'm really nervous.

111
00:04:16.525 --> 00:04:17.442
Of course.

112
00:04:17.722 --> 00:04:19.237
How long is your slot?

113
00:04:19.517 --> 00:04:20.647
Five minutes.

114
00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.485
I've got twelve slides.

115
00:04:22.765 --> 00:04:23.895
That's too many.

116
00:04:24.175 --> 00:04:26.629
What are the three things you want people to remember?

117
00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:29.579
The conversation continues.

118
00:04:29.859 --> 00:04:36.387
That deploys are faster now, that outages are down, and that we need two more people for on-call.

119
00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:37.712
Perfect.

120
00:04:37.992 --> 00:04:40.275
One slide each, plus a title slide.

121
00:04:40.555 --> 00:04:42.155
How are you going to start?

122
00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:45.272
I was going to introduce myself and the team.

123
00:04:45.552 --> 00:04:47.280
Start with a number instead.

124
00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:48.989
Something surprising.

125
00:04:50.339 --> 00:04:51.939
The conversation continues.

126
00:04:52.219 --> 00:04:54.715
Last year, a deploy took two hours.

127
00:04:54.995 --> 00:04:56.915
Today it takes eighteen minutes.

128
00:04:57.195 --> 00:04:58.646
That's a great opening!

129
00:04:58.926 --> 00:05:01.465
Practise it five times tonight, and you'll be fine.

130
00:05:01.745 --> 00:05:02.875
Thanks, Meena.

131
00:05:03.155 --> 00:05:04.457
That really helps.

132
00:05:05.807 --> 00:05:06.681
Part 8.

133
00:05:06.961 --> 00:05:07.879
Practice.

134
00:05:09.129 --> 00:05:16.297
Prepare a 60-second talk on My favourite tool at work, using: hook, then point, then three reasons, then close.

135
00:05:16.577 --> 00:05:19.990
Record it, then watch it once and note one thing to improve.

136
00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.236
Part 9.

137
00:05:22.516 --> 00:05:23.561
Speak today.

138
00:05:24.811 --> 00:05:27.457
Give your 60-second talk out loud three times.

139
00:05:27.737 --> 00:05:31.833
Count your filler words in the recording, and try again with pauses instead.

140
00:05:32.113 --> 00:05:37.083
Volunteer for one small speaking moment this week (stand-up summary, demo, toast).

141
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:39.969
That's the end of this lesson.

142
00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:44.921
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

143
00:05:45.201 --> 00:05:51.303
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

